    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d223">

                <pb facs="0313" n="229" xml:id="pg229"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72114/3660</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-06-04T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 4, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8075. Ascertain and cable as soon as possible attitude of British Government
                    towards status of British officers and crews of British or foreign armed
                    merchant vessels who have been captured and interned by Germans. Does British
                    Government acknowledge German right of capture and regard these men as prisoners
                    of war? Does Government or employers pay them salaries or relief, and do
                    salaries date from time of original capture on high sea or date of landing as
                    prisoners? What amounts are paid respectively to officers and men? Report fully
                    any arrangements made in this connection.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
